1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,449
Back to our puzzle:

2
00:00:01,449 --> 00:00:05,568
two projects, two versions of httpx,
one shared shelf.

3
00:00:05,888 --> 00:00:09,024
The fix is to give
each project its own toolbox.

4
00:00:09,344 --> 00:00:11,883
That toolbox is a virtual environment.

5
00:00:12,117 --> 00:00:17,685
A virtual environment is just a folder,
usually called dot venv, with three parts.

6
00:00:18,005 --> 00:00:20,693
First, a bin folder with a python inside.

7
00:00:21,013 --> 00:00:25,663
It's usually not a copy, just a link
to a real interpreter somewhere else.

8
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:30,188
Second, its own site-packages folder,
empty when uv makes it.

9
00:00:30,508 --> 00:00:35,905
Site-packages is the usual name;
Debian calls its system ones dist-packages.

10
00:00:36,225 --> 00:00:40,172
This is where this project's packages go,
and only this project's.

11
00:00:40,417 --> 00:00:44,044
Third, a small config file, pyvenv.cfg.

12
00:00:44,364 --> 00:00:47,157
When Python starts
from the venv's bin folder,

13
00:00:47,157 --> 00:00:48,310
it reads this file.

14
00:00:48,630 --> 00:00:51,797
It says: you're in a venv,
so use my site-packages,

15
00:00:51,797 --> 00:00:53,473
and leave the system's out.

16
00:00:53,717 --> 00:00:55,530
Here's the difference on the shelves.

17
00:00:55,850 --> 00:00:58,986
Started from the venv,
Python builds a different list.

18
00:00:59,306 --> 00:01:03,616
The standard library still comes
from the real interpreter it links to.

19
00:01:03,936 --> 00:01:07,218
But both dist-packages shelves
from the system are gone,

20
00:01:07,218 --> 00:01:09,738
and the venv's own shelf takes their place.

21
00:01:09,983 --> 00:01:12,031
Same engine, different toolbox.

22
00:01:12,351 --> 00:01:14,176
And the toolboxes don't mix:

23
00:01:14,176 --> 00:01:17,827
packages in the venv
are invisible to the system Python,

24
00:01:17,827 --> 00:01:19,391
and the other way round.

25
00:01:19,633 --> 00:01:20,934
Remember pip's hint?

26
00:01:21,254 --> 00:01:25,628
It suggested python3 dash m venv,
the classic way to make one.

27
00:01:25,948 --> 00:01:28,892
Today, uv makes it for us,
and does a lot more.

28
00:01:29,133 --> 00:01:32,585
What a venv doesn't isolate:
the operating system,

29
00:01:32,585 --> 00:01:35,554
system libraries written in C, and drivers.

30
00:01:35,874 --> 00:01:41,123
Those are jobs for a whole machine,
like our VM, or a container, next lecture.

31
00:01:41,367 --> 00:01:45,847
After uv sync, we'll open the real folder
and find all three parts.

32
00:01:47,683 --> 00:01:52,526
Part 2 starts on a fresh VM,
same settings, and the meter runs again.

33
00:01:52,846 --> 00:01:56,024
Fresh matters:
this system Python never sees pip.

34
00:01:56,344 --> 00:01:59,550
Create it and SSH in; if the name is taken,

35
00:01:59,550 --> 00:02:03,427
the old VM is still being deleted,
so wait a minute.

36
00:02:03,747 --> 00:02:05,966
Pause until your terminal is open.

37
00:02:06,216 --> 00:02:11,293
Then the same four commands:
apt-get update, install git, clone, and cd.

38
00:02:11,613 --> 00:02:14,536
Pause until your prompt ends
in simple-translate.

39
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:17,467
Step 7 installs uv in one line.

40
00:02:17,787 --> 00:02:20,010
First, curl downloads a script:

41
00:02:20,010 --> 00:02:23,666
it follows redirects,
stays quiet but shows errors,

42
00:02:23,666 --> 00:02:28,837
and fails on an HTTP error instead
of handing an error page to the shell.

43
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:30,825
The vertical bar is a pipe:

44
00:02:30,825 --> 00:02:36,189
it hands the script straight to the shell,
so you're running code from the internet:

45
00:02:36,189 --> 00:02:39,382
uv's official installer,
which you can read first.

46
00:02:39,633 --> 00:02:40,785
Pause and run it.

47
00:02:41,105 --> 00:02:46,210
It installs uv,
version 0.13.0 at the time of recording,

48
00:02:46,210 --> 00:02:51,132
and uvx, into dot local slash bin
in your home folder.

49
00:02:51,383 --> 00:02:53,751
Let's try uv, with an extra check.

50
00:02:54,071 --> 00:02:55,308
Command not found.

51
00:02:55,628 --> 00:02:57,207
But we just installed it.

52
00:02:57,466 --> 00:02:58,931
When you type a command,

53
00:02:58,931 --> 00:03:01,737
the shell walks a list
of folders called PATH,

54
00:03:01,737 --> 00:03:04,143
left to right, and runs the first match.

55
00:03:04,463 --> 00:03:05,573
None contains uv,

56
00:03:05,573 --> 00:03:08,837
and the shell only knows
the PATH it started with.

57
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:12,797
The installer's last lines,
and step 8, give two options:

58
00:03:12,797 --> 00:03:15,521
reload your shell, or source the env file.

59
00:03:15,841 --> 00:03:19,907
A reload means a new SSH window,
back in your home folder,

60
00:03:19,907 --> 00:03:22,711
so you'd cd into simple-translate again.

61
00:03:23,031 --> 00:03:26,508
Sourcing is quicker:
it puts that folder first in PATH.

62
00:03:26,828 --> 00:03:28,705
Pause and run the source line.

63
00:03:28,966 --> 00:03:30,521
Then two extra checks:

64
00:03:30,521 --> 00:03:34,833
uv reports its version,
and which uv points into that folder.

65
00:03:35,100 --> 00:03:36,849
Why source, and not sh?

66
00:03:37,169 --> 00:03:40,868
Plain sh would change a child shell
that exits at once;

67
00:03:40,868 --> 00:03:42,481
source changes this one.

68
00:03:42,801 --> 00:03:46,968
The installer also made
your startup files run this env file,

69
00:03:46,968 --> 00:03:50,246
so next time you connect,
uv is found by itself.

70
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:51,759
So what is uv?

71
00:03:52,079 --> 00:03:55,999
One fast tool that replaces several:
the venv module,

72
00:03:55,999 --> 00:04:00,953
pip, pip-tools for locking,
pyenv for installing Pythons, and pipx.

73
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:06,239
Step 9 is uv sync,
and it reads pyproject.toml,

74
00:04:06,239 --> 00:04:08,117
so let's read it first.

75
00:04:08,437 --> 00:04:09,525
The name is app.

76
00:04:09,845 --> 00:04:17,397
Two dependencies: art, version 6.5 or newer,
and googletrans, exactly 4.0.2.

77
00:04:17,717 --> 00:04:21,387
Greater-or-equal means a range;
double equals, one version.

78
00:04:21,707 --> 00:04:25,867
Line 6 is a range again:
Python 3.13 or newer.

79
00:04:26,117 --> 00:04:27,162
Now predict.

80
00:04:27,482 --> 00:04:29,957
This VM has Python 3.11.

81
00:04:30,277 --> 00:04:33,136
The project asks for 3.13 or newer.

82
00:04:33,456 --> 00:04:34,949
What will uv sync do?

83
00:04:35,269 --> 00:04:38,703
Fail, use 3.11 anyway, or something else?

84
00:04:47,550 --> 00:04:49,257
Pause and run uv sync.

85
00:04:49,577 --> 00:04:53,823
Instead of failing,
uv downloads its own Python:

86
00:04:53,823 --> 00:04:57,449
CPython 3.15.0, at the time of recording.

87
00:04:57,769 --> 00:05:01,033
Then it creates a virtual environment
in dot venv.

88
00:05:01,353 --> 00:05:06,963
If uv can't find a pyproject.toml,
cd into simple-translate first.

89
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:08,437
Here's what happened.

90
00:05:08,757 --> 00:05:11,296
Picture Python versions on a number line.

91
00:05:11,616 --> 00:05:15,499
The constraint lights up everything
from 3.13 onward.

92
00:05:15,819 --> 00:05:18,994
First, uv looks
for an interpreter it already has:

93
00:05:18,994 --> 00:05:21,344
its own downloads, then the system's.

94
00:05:21,664 --> 00:05:26,741
Debian's 3.11.2 lands outside the range,
so it can't be used.

95
00:05:26,983 --> 00:05:29,863
So uv downloads one. Which one?

96
00:05:30,183 --> 00:05:34,427
Not 3.13,
but the newest release in the range,

97
00:05:34,427 --> 00:05:37,564
which was 3.15.0 when we recorded.

98
00:05:37,884 --> 00:05:42,471
It lives in uv's own folder in your home,
and the venv's python links to it.

99
00:05:42,717 --> 00:05:45,405
That's the catch: a constraint is not a pin.

100
00:05:45,725 --> 00:05:51,336
Before Python 3.15 came out,
this same command gave people 3.14.

101
00:05:51,656 --> 00:05:55,303
Watch this video next year,
and you may get something newer still.

102
00:05:55,550 --> 00:05:58,238
On Debian 13, the story changes.

103
00:05:58,558 --> 00:06:03,635
Its system Python, 3.13.5,
is already inside the range.

104
00:06:03,955 --> 00:06:06,568
So uv simply uses it, with no download,

105
00:06:06,568 --> 00:06:09,651
and the venv's python links
to the system one.

106
00:06:09,971 --> 00:06:12,958
Same command,
different Debian, different Python.

107
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:15,739
To get the same Python everywhere, pin it.

108
00:06:16,059 --> 00:06:20,162
The command uv python pin 3.13,
not in the README,

109
00:06:20,162 --> 00:06:23,937
writes a small file
called dot python version,

110
00:06:23,937 --> 00:06:27,301
and the range shrinks to the 3.13 series.

111
00:06:27,621 --> 00:06:31,032
Don't run it now:
your next uv sync would rebuild the venv,

112
00:06:31,032 --> 00:06:33,403
and the rest of the video wouldn't match.

113
00:06:33,723 --> 00:06:35,323
It's in the exercises.

114
00:06:36,567 --> 00:06:37,890
Now the packages.

115
00:06:38,210 --> 00:06:42,212
A quick puzzle:
pyproject.toml lists 2 dependencies,

116
00:06:42,212 --> 00:06:46,060
and in part 1,
pip installed 12 packages for them.

117
00:06:46,380 --> 00:06:48,044
How many will uv install?

118
00:06:54,884 --> 00:06:57,337
It's 11: one fewer than part 1.

119
00:06:57,657 --> 00:07:03,289
It resolved 13 and installed 11,
each with a plus sign and an exact version.

120
00:07:03,609 --> 00:07:06,937
So where did the other 9 come from,
and which one went missing?

121
00:07:07,184 --> 00:07:12,816
Packages have dependencies of their own;
this extra command, uv tree, draws them.

122
00:07:13,136 --> 00:07:17,626
Our googletrans needs httpx,
an HTTP library,

123
00:07:17,626 --> 00:07:22,715
which needs anyio,
certifi, httpcore, h2, and more.

124
00:07:23,035 --> 00:07:24,890
Two wishes grow into a tree.

125
00:07:25,134 --> 00:07:29,686
Resolving means choosing one exact version
for every package in the tree,

126
00:07:29,686 --> 00:07:31,619
versions that all fit together.

127
00:07:31,939 --> 00:07:38,019
Then uv writes them to a new file, uv.lock,
with download addresses and checksums.

128
00:07:38,339 --> 00:07:41,454
The pyproject.toml file is your wish list.

129
00:07:41,774 --> 00:07:44,248
The uv.lock file is the receipt.

130
00:07:44,484 --> 00:07:46,297
So why 13 resolved?

131
00:07:46,617 --> 00:07:51,076
It's 11 packages, plus the app itself,
which uv doesn't download.

132
00:07:51,396 --> 00:07:53,228
The 13th is in uv.lock,

133
00:07:53,228 --> 00:07:58,009
which covers every Python
the project allows, not just ours.

134
00:07:58,329 --> 00:08:04,473
In it, anyio needs typing extensions,
but only on Python older than 3.15.

135
00:08:04,717 --> 00:08:07,789
On our 3.15, it's skipped, so 11.

136
00:08:08,109 --> 00:08:12,760
Part 1 ran on Python 3.11,
so pip installed it: 12.

137
00:08:13,080 --> 00:08:18,520
And on Debian 13, with Python 3.13,
uv installs 12 too.

138
00:08:18,767 --> 00:08:20,538
Here's why the receipt matters.

139
00:08:20,858 --> 00:08:23,546
Two students run uv sync a week apart.

140
00:08:23,866 --> 00:08:25,122
Without a shared lock,

141
00:08:25,122 --> 00:08:29,007
each resolves fresh and may get
different versions deep in the tree.

142
00:08:29,327 --> 00:08:33,530
With the same uv.lock,
they get exactly the same packages.

143
00:08:33,767 --> 00:08:35,090
Two extra checks.

144
00:08:35,410 --> 00:08:41,042
First, ls dash a shows two new things:
the dot venv folder, and uv.lock.

145
00:08:41,362 --> 00:08:44,498
And git status says uv.lock is untracked.

146
00:08:44,818 --> 00:08:47,442
It was made here;
it didn't come with the repo.

147
00:08:47,684 --> 00:08:51,076
Now, a small mistake in the README,
with an easy fix.

148
00:08:51,396 --> 00:08:55,343
It says uv.lock gives
the next person the same versions.

149
00:08:55,663 --> 00:09:00,249
But the repo never committed one,
so each student's first sync resolves fresh.

150
00:09:00,569 --> 00:09:03,449
In your own projects, commit uv.lock.

151
00:09:03,769 --> 00:09:07,725
In automated builds,
run uv sync with the locked flag,

152
00:09:07,725 --> 00:09:10,510
which refuses a missing or stale lock.

153
00:09:10,750 --> 00:09:13,481
Last, an extra: run uv sync again.

154
00:09:13,801 --> 00:09:18,595
It checks the same packages, 11 here,
in a fraction of a millisecond,

155
00:09:18,595 --> 00:09:21,652
and changes nothing: safe to re-run anytime.

156
00:09:22,900 --> 00:09:25,055
Let's open the venv, as promised.

157
00:09:25,375 --> 00:09:28,214
This extra command lists its bin folder:

158
00:09:28,214 --> 00:09:32,756
python, a link to the Python uv downloaded,
and, notice, no pip.

159
00:09:33,700 --> 00:09:37,770
And the config file:
home is the real interpreter's folder,

160
00:09:37,770 --> 00:09:41,977
include system site packages is false,
and the prompt is app.

161
00:09:42,297 --> 00:09:44,111
All three parts, as drawn.

162
00:09:44,350 --> 00:09:48,019
Step 10:
source dot venv slash bin slash activate.

163
00:09:48,339 --> 00:09:49,491
Pause and run it.

164
00:09:49,811 --> 00:09:52,691
Your prompt now starts with app,
in parentheses.

165
00:09:53,011 --> 00:09:55,529
The README's example says simple-translate.

166
00:09:55,849 --> 00:10:01,161
But uv names the prompt after the project,
and pyproject.toml calls it app.

167
00:10:01,400 --> 00:10:02,381
What changed?

168
00:10:02,701 --> 00:10:07,160
Before activating, python knocked
on every door in PATH and found nothing.

169
00:10:07,480 --> 00:10:10,068
The activate script saves your old PATH,

170
00:10:10,068 --> 00:10:15,309
then puts the bin folder of the venv first,
so python is found at the first door.

171
00:10:15,550 --> 00:10:20,242
Extra checks:
which python points into dot venv,

172
00:10:20,242 --> 00:10:25,394
and python reports 3.15.0,
at the time of recording;

173
00:10:25,394 --> 00:10:27,475
on Debian 13, 3.13.5.

174
00:10:27,795 --> 00:10:29,438
And which pip finds nothing.

175
00:10:30,333 --> 00:10:32,883
Another small mistake in the README:

176
00:10:32,883 --> 00:10:37,842
it says activation points pip into the venv,
but a uv venv has no pip.

177
00:10:38,162 --> 00:10:42,279
On a machine with a system pip,
which pip finds that one instead,

178
00:10:42,279 --> 00:10:44,306
and it refuses, as at the start.

179
00:10:44,626 --> 00:10:47,200
To add a package, use uv add,

180
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:51,816
which also records it
in pyproject.toml and uv.lock.

181
00:10:52,050 --> 00:10:55,443
Activation also sets
a variable called virtual env,

182
00:10:55,443 --> 00:10:56,723
changes the prompt,

183
00:10:56,723 --> 00:11:00,498
and adds a command called deactivate
that undoes it all.

184
00:11:00,818 --> 00:11:04,551
It only changes where the shell looks first,
and only in this shell.

185
00:11:04,871 --> 00:11:10,397
If your SSH window reconnects,
cd into simple-translate and activate again.

186
00:11:10,683 --> 00:11:14,459
Step 11: the same three runs,
now with plain python.

187
00:11:14,779 --> 00:11:18,079
Copy the long ones from the README,
quotes included,

188
00:11:18,079 --> 00:11:19,792
and pause to run all three.

189
00:11:20,112 --> 00:11:23,632
This time Google answered:
these are real translations.

190
00:11:23,952 --> 00:11:28,945
If you get a 429 again
but see the Input line, your venv works.

191
00:11:29,183 --> 00:11:32,511
Two extras, not in the README,
that skip activation.

192
00:11:32,831 --> 00:11:39,572
First, uv run python cli.py syncs the venv
if needed, then runs the app inside it.

193
00:11:39,892 --> 00:11:42,069
Or call the venv's python by its path.

194
00:11:42,317 --> 00:11:45,304
And why source,
not just run the activate script?

195
00:11:45,624 --> 00:11:48,732
It isn't even executable,
and run as a program,

196
00:11:48,732 --> 00:11:53,559
it would only change a child shell
that exits at once, like the env file.

197
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:57,424
Let's prove the isolation with a few extras.

198
00:11:57,744 --> 00:12:02,715
Deactivate first: while the venv is active,
even python3 points into it.

199
00:12:03,035 --> 00:12:06,789
Then ask the system's
python3 to import googletrans.

200
00:12:07,109 --> 00:12:08,709
ModuleNotFoundError.

201
00:12:09,029 --> 00:12:12,400
On this VM,
the packages exist only in the venv.

202
00:12:12,633 --> 00:12:17,092
On the shelves: the system Python
scans its own list, top to bottom.

203
00:12:17,412 --> 00:12:21,209
The venv's shelf isn't on that list,
so the search comes up empty.

204
00:12:21,533 --> 00:12:26,162
Now the same import through uv run,
printing where this Python thinks it lives.

205
00:12:26,482 --> 00:12:29,277
It works,
and the answer is our dot venv folder.

206
00:12:29,517 --> 00:12:32,162
Now imagine you'd kept your part-1 VM.

207
00:12:32,482 --> 00:12:36,599
Same import, system python3, no venv.
Does it work?

208
00:12:43,433 --> 00:12:49,289
Yes: on that VM, sudo pip put googletrans
on the system-wide user local shelf,

209
00:12:49,289 --> 00:12:51,241
so every program finds it.

210
00:12:51,561 --> 00:12:54,377
Same import,
different shelves, different answer.

211
00:12:54,617 --> 00:12:56,366
And it works the other way too.

212
00:12:56,686 --> 00:13:02,145
Even on that messy part-1 VM, a venv
would ignore everything sudo pip put there,

213
00:13:02,145 --> 00:13:04,942
because those shelves aren't on its list.

214
00:13:05,262 --> 00:13:07,438
That's the whole lesson in one picture.

215
00:13:09,083 --> 00:13:13,584
One more tool you'll meet:
pipenv, an older way to do the same job.

216
00:13:13,904 --> 00:13:18,662
This repo's pipenv branch,
an older version of this tutorial, uses it.

217
00:13:18,982 --> 00:13:24,486
So do the separate AWS version and,
later, the cheese app's API service.

218
00:13:24,733 --> 00:13:26,653
Same ideas, different names.

219
00:13:26,973 --> 00:13:33,629
The pyproject.toml file becomes the Pipfile,
and uv.lock becomes Pipfile.lock.

220
00:13:33,949 --> 00:13:37,960
The venv lives in a folder
in your home instead of in the project.

221
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:42,568
The command uv sync becomes pipenv install,
or pipenv sync.

222
00:13:42,888 --> 00:13:44,552
And instead of activating,

223
00:13:44,552 --> 00:13:47,560
pipenv shell opens
a new shell inside the venv.

224
00:13:47,880 --> 00:13:49,394
Type exit to leave it.

225
00:13:49,633 --> 00:13:51,894
And here's our puzzle again, for real.

226
00:13:52,214 --> 00:13:56,738
That branch's lock file pins httpx 0.13.3,

227
00:13:56,738 --> 00:14:03,308
while our uv sync installed 0.28.1,
at the time of recording.

228
00:14:03,628 --> 00:14:07,553
Two versions of the same app,
two versions of httpx.

229
00:14:07,873 --> 00:14:11,137
On one shared system Python,
they couldn't live together.

230
00:14:11,457 --> 00:14:12,929
In two venvs, they can.

231
00:14:15,967 --> 00:14:19,182
Step 12: delete the VM, with its boot disk,

232
00:14:19,182 --> 00:14:23,220
then check that VM instances
and Disks are both empty.

233
00:14:23,540 --> 00:14:25,951
Pause and do it now, before you forget.

234
00:14:26,200 --> 00:14:27,437
Here's where we are.

235
00:14:27,757 --> 00:14:31,661
The VM gave us a whole machine
that we can wreck and delete.

236
00:14:31,981 --> 00:14:36,056
The venv keeps one project's packages away
from that machine's Python.

237
00:14:36,376 --> 00:14:39,278
And between the two,
there's room for one more layer.

238
00:14:39,517 --> 00:14:42,440
That layer is a container,
and it's next lecture.

239
00:14:42,760 --> 00:14:47,231
The tutorial's Dockerfile already
shows the idea: copy the code,

240
00:14:47,231 --> 00:14:48,720
run the same uv sync,

241
00:14:48,720 --> 00:14:51,912
and start in a shell
with the venv activated.

242
00:14:52,232 --> 00:14:54,984
Everything you did today,
baked into an image.

243
00:14:55,234 --> 00:14:57,197
One thing to watch for next time.

244
00:14:57,517 --> 00:15:01,322
The Dockerfile starts
from a Python 3.12 image,

245
00:15:01,322 --> 00:15:04,237
but the project wants 3.13 or newer.

246
00:15:04,557 --> 00:15:07,052
You already know what uv will do about that.

247
00:15:07,300 --> 00:15:10,329
Before you go,
five questions to test yourself.

248
00:15:10,649 --> 00:15:14,553
One: what does the break-system-packages
flag actually break,

249
00:15:14,553 --> 00:15:16,986
and where did sudo pip3 put the files?

250
00:15:17,234 --> 00:15:20,285
Two: name the three parts
of a virtual environment.

251
00:15:20,605 --> 00:15:23,195
Three: what exactly does activation change,

252
00:15:23,195 --> 00:15:26,087
and what are two ways
to run the app without it?

253
00:15:26,334 --> 00:15:29,534
Four: 2 dependencies became 11 packages.

254
00:15:29,854 --> 00:15:32,883
Why, and which file makes
everyone get the same ones?

255
00:15:33,203 --> 00:15:36,610
Five:
is requires Python 3.13 or newer a pin,

256
00:15:36,610 --> 00:15:40,094
and what would make
the version deterministic?

257
00:15:40,334 --> 00:15:42,510
The answers are in the exercises.

258
00:15:42,830 --> 00:15:45,960
Every command from today
is in the study guide,

259
00:15:45,960 --> 00:15:47,758
in the MLOps Learning repo.

260
00:15:48,078 --> 00:15:49,784
Next time: containers.

